🚀 Python Basics – Operators & Number System Conversion
🔹 1. Operators in Python

Python provides different types of operators:

➡️ Arithmetic Operators

Used for basic mathematical operations.
a = 10
b = 3

print(a + b)   # Addition → 13
print(a - b)   # Subtraction → 7
print(a * b)   # Multiplication → 30
print(a / b)   # Division → 3.333...
print(a % b)   # Modulus (remainder) → 1
print(a ** b)  # Exponentiation → 1000
print(a // b)  # Floor Division → 3

➡️ Comparison (Relational) Operators

Used to compare values (returns True/False).
x, y = 5, 10

print(x == y)  # Equal → False
print(x != y)  # Not equal → True
print(x > y)   # Greater than → False
print(x < y)   # Less than → True
print(x >= y)  # Greater or equal → False
print(x <= y)  # Less or equal → True

➡️ Logical Operators

Used with conditional statements.
p, q = True, False

print(p and q)  # AND → False
print(p or q)   # OR → True
print(not p)    # NOT → False

➡️ Assignment Operators

Used to assign values.
num = 10
num += 5   # num = num + 5 → 15
num -= 3   # num = num - 3 → 12
num *= 2   # num = num * 2 → 24
num /= 4   # num = num / 4 → 6.0

➡️ Bitwise Operators

Work on bits (0 and 1).
a = 6   # binary: 110
b = 3   # binary: 011

print(a & b)  # AND → 2 (010)
print(a | b)  # OR  → 7 (111)
print(a ^ b)  # XOR → 5 (101)
print(~a)     # NOT → -7 (inverts bits)
print(a << 1) # Left Shift → 12 (1100)
print(a >> 1) # Right Shift → 3 (011)

🔹 2. Number System Conversion
➡️ Decimal → Binary
num = 13
binary = bin(num)[2:]  # remove '0b'
print(binary)  # 1101

➡️ Binary → Decimal
binary_str = "1101"
decimal = int(binary_str, 2)
print(decimal)  # 13

If stored as list of bits:
bits_list = [1, 1, 0, 1]
decimal = int("".join(str(bit) for bit in bits_list), 2)
print(decimal)  # 13
